<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tanzimat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tanzimat was a period of reform in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from 1839 to 1876. Prompted by growing European military and economic superiority, it sought to modernise administration, law, taxation and the army, and proclaimed the equality of Ottoman subjects regardless of religion. Accounts of the Ottoman Empire present it as a major attempt to strengthen the state from within, culminating in the first Ottoman constitution of 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanzimat.jpg" alt="Tanzimat" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juris Tiltins · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Ottoman_Empire_(1882%E2%80%931922).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Tanzimat</b> reforms of 1839–1876, prompted by growing European military and economic superiority, sought to modernise administration and law, proclaiming equality before the law regardless of religion, reorganising the provinces and founding secular schools, and culminated in the short-lived constitution of 1876.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its users called it lisân-ı Türkî or zebân-ı Türkî, “the Turkish language”, or lisân-ı Rûm, “the language of Rum”, and more generally Türkçe or Türkî, “Turkish”; only in the <b>Tanzimat</b> era (1839–1876) did the name “Ottoman”, lisân-ı Osmânî or Osmanlıca, come into use.</blockquote><a href="/language/ottoman-turkish">Ottoman Turkish · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Ottoman Empire the <b>Tanzimat</b> reforms, from 1839, promised equality to subjects of every religion.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The CUP’s drive for centralization continued an effort begun with the <b>Tanzimat</b> reforms.</blockquote><a href="/movement/young-turks">Young Turks · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Attempts to stave off the empire’s collapse began chiefly with the 19th-century <b>Tanzimat</b> reforms; the Young Ottomans of the mid-century tried to build an Ottoman nationalism, with limited democracy, against rising ethnic nationalism, and after their demise under Sultan Abdul Hamid II’s absolutist reign the Young Turks took…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/kemalism">Kemalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Ottoman Empire and the Middle East liberalism influenced the <b>Tanzimat</b> and Al-Nahda reforms, the Young Ottomans and Islamic Modernism, though its books and papers reached mainly intellectuals and the emerging middle class.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
